  "prompt_text": "The figure compares three common ways fibrous or polymer-like building blocks can be organized in a material, highlighting how different internal architectures look.\n\nOn the left, an “oriented structure” shows long green strands mostly aligned in one direction between repeating blue vertical domains, with arrows indicating a common orientation. Small yellow circles are dispersed among the strands, suggesting additional components embedded within the aligned network. Overall, this panel emphasizes directional order.\n\nIn the middle, the “chiral nematic structure” is drawn as stacked short rods whose orientation gradually rotates from one layer to the next, producing repeating spindle-like columns. This conveys a helicoidal arrangement: local alignment exists, but the preferred direction twists periodically through space.\n\nOn the right, the “brick–mortar structure” shows pink horizontal plate-like layers separated by a wavy, interconnecting green matrix. Unlike the first two, this architecture combines rigid, brick-like lamellae with a softer continuous phase filling the gaps, giving a layered composite appearance.\n\nTaken together, the figure illustrates three distinct structural motifs—aligned, helically twisted, and layered composite—that likely lead to different mechanical or functional properties because their internal order and connectivity are very different.",
